Found the bug! Line 182-185:

```python
if syms != var:
    dict_sym_index = dict(zip(syms, range(len(syms))))
    return {tuple([t[dict_sym_index[i]] for i in var])
                for t in diophantine(eq, param)}
```

The recursive call `diophantine(eq, param)` drops `permute=True` (and any other params). So when `syms != var` (i.e., user's syms order differs from sorted default order), the recursion solves WITHOUT permute, then just reorders the tuple elements. That's why `syms=(n,m)` gives only the base solution {(3,2)} reordered, while `syms=(m,n)` (matching sorted var order) goes through the full path with permute.

The fix: pass `permute` (and ideally `param`) through the recursive call:

```python
return {tuple([t[dict_sym_index[i]] for i in var])
            for t in diophantine(eq, param, permute=permute)}
```

